{"skill":{"slug":"guicountrol","displayName":"Linux GUI  Control"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1769949980822,"changelog":"linux-gui-control 1.0.0 initial release\n\n- Automate Linux desktop interactions using xdotool, wmctrl, and dogtail.\n- Supports simulating mouse/keyboard input, window management, and text-based UI inspection on X11/GNOME.\n- Offers scripts for automating clicks, typing, window focus, and window movement.\n- Enables extraction of application UI hierarchies for accessible apps and provides screenshot support for visual tasks.","changelogSource":"auto","license":null,"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":2126,"sha256":"fe0194a1fa96066faa523d13a520878533ad65a662119a42e2577b8f03cda37c","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/gui_action.sh","size":656,"sha256":"c8261407a07b10893da06a8f484f240525efd1a6cc43c0ddd96af79d4762af1f","contentType":"application/x-shellscript"},{"path":"scripts/inspect_ui.py","size":1201,"sha256":"cefdb1e7c81fe23967f8ea098ac1b4ea13102c450cb6f6fc866bc429b517eb6a","contentType":"text/x-python"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2350,"sha256":"2d9f58860d1ed7190d831513da47386a96d545a05d2c2e429d7668f6afbc2d4e","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789026442671,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"1ca3f482ad0b9d8047e655cf7f856b4956fffd912abeb777d9d0232093ca8b6d","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/1ca3f482ad0b9d8047e655cf7f856b4956fffd912abeb777d9d0232093ca8b6d","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: guicountrol\nVersion: 1.0.0\n\nThe skill bundle is classified as suspicious due to its powerful capabilities for Linux GUI control, including simulating mouse/keyboard input, managing windows, taking screenshots, and killing processes, as demonstrated by `xdotool`, `wmctrl`, `scrot`, `pkill`, and `dogtail` usage in `SKILL.md`, `scripts/gui_action.sh`, and `scripts/inspect_ui.py`. While these actions are plausibly aligned with the stated purpose of 'Linux GUI Control', they represent significant high-risk capabilities that could be misused if the agent's instructions or environment were compromised, even though no explicit malicious intent is present in the provided files.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160664494},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":38,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":6,"checkedAt":1789026442671},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This skill mostly does what it says, but needs review because its screenshot helper can be abused to run commands and its GUI controls can act broadly on the live desktop without clear safeguards.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Use of xdotool, wmctrl, dogtail, and scrot is coherent for X11/GNOME GUI control, but these tools can interact with unrelated applications and capture sensitive on-screen content.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"note"},{"detail":"No install-time execution, package installation hook, dependency download, or startup registration was found in the inspected artifacts.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The instructions authorize arbitrary live desktop input and window activation with little target verification, confirmation, or safety guidance, and include pkill/nohup relaunch steps that can affect active application sessions.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill suggests relaunching apps with nohup in the background and killing processes with pkill; this is not a backdoor or service installation, but it lacks cleanup and data-loss guidance.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Linux GUI automation, UI inspection, typing/clicking, and screenshots match the stated purpose, but the screenshot wrapper passes a caller-controlled filename to scrot without option termination or validation, allowing behavior beyond ordinary screenshot capture.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Review before installing. Use only in a desktop session where broad click/type/window control is acceptable, save work before following pkill instructions, avoid using untrusted text as action arguments, and patch or avoid the screenshot helper until it validates filenames and passes an end-of-options marker to scrot.","findings":"[SQP-2] expected: The live click/type/key/activate and screenshot capabilities are expected for a GUI-control skill, but the artifacts under-disclose risks around focus mistakes, sensitive screenshots, and destructive desktop actions.\n[RA2] expected: The nohup example is purpose-aligned for enabling Electron accessibility and does not show covert persistence, but it can leave a modified app instance running without documented cleanup.\n[T09] unexpected: The static finding is supported by artifact code: scripts/gui_action.sh runs scrot -z \"$1\" without rejecting option-shaped filenames or using an end-of-options marker, which can let a crafted screenshot filename change scrot behavior.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789026442671}}}}}